{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as an operational internal time source for the cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 was persistently configured as a chrony stratum-8 local source, allowed the cluster subnet, listened on UDP/123, and remained enabled, active, healthy, and reachable through the firewall after reboot. Final client observations show node2 making NTP requests.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3dc35dd6c85b4a2a","cmd-11ed8fe999264987","cmd-d70b36f6f49141e2","cmd-2979b426bf824ff8","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node2 to synchronize its clock against node1 instead of public time servers.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2's configuration contains only node1 at 10.100.246.10 as its time server. Final post-reboot observations directly show node1 selected as ^*, node2 at stratum 9 with Normal leap status, and a current sub-millisecond offset.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fa2dccef98d4052","cmd-cf34e842b4514e02","cmd-040a882582174402","cmd-2979b426bf824ff8"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL Server 7.9 with kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64. The preinstalled RHEL 7 chrony 3.4 package was used, with no package replacement or OS/kernel change shown.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1eaf920c67994184","cmd-9c932774079c4f93","cmd-040a882582174402","cmd-2979b426bf824ff8","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Required mutations were narrowly related to configuring chrony, opening NTP on node1, and performing the mandated restart checks. One unnecessary, bounded troubleshooting mutation occurred on node2: the runtime makestep/burst attempt did not restore selection and was superseded by restarting chronyd; it did not remain after the later node2 reboot (cmd-5f833544d5f04cbc). The necessary reboot commands also left /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log on both nodes; those small files remained and were not cleaned up (cmd-168e4d7f5ff14e1d, cmd-f28cb8d6cffd42e3). No failed services or other unexpected sockets were observed afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5f833544d5f04cbc","cmd-4d3db83c29d647a5","cmd-168e4d7f5ff14e1d","cmd-f28cb8d6cffd42e3","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was completed for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged reboot scheduling, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/configuration/time state. The node1 disruption initially left node2 with stale/degraded source data, but the bounded recovery sequence ultimately restored node2 synchronization, and both nodes then passed their final reboot-persistence checks.","limitations":["Native package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package-manager consistency could not be independently assessed.","The restart acknowledgement is the executor-observed reboot scheduling response rather than a separate provider control-plane acknowledgement; changed boot identities and short post-boot uptimes nevertheless directly confirm both restarts.","Lifecycle snapshots cover platform, boot identity, sockets, temporary files, failed services, and package-health attempts, but not the chrony configuration itself; final chrony state is established by executor command evidence."]}
